WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:07.600
At two fifty-eight in the afternoon, the fleet put twelve hundred and twenty-eight watts into one house.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.200
All of it heat.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.000
Each Linux machine writes a line of its readings every five seconds.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.600
The air of the house is read by nothing.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.200
The nearest thing to a room thermometer is a disk in saskatchewan, at twenty-eight.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.000
The room is no warmer than that.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.400
How much cooler is not known.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Here is why that matters.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:41.000
A watt the house needed anyway is a watt of computing that cost nothing more than the heating it replaced.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.400
On a cold night, against resistance heat, the computation is free.

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.800
Against a heat pump, it costs two thirds of its electricity.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:56.200
On a warm day it costs all of it, and more if anything cools the house.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.400
This is Orrery's Heat, the Watts panel, that afternoon:

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.200
each machine's share of the fleet's heat.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.200
And one line above them.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.000
The room:

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.200
read by nothing.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:12.000
Three mesh radios were on this Mac.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.400
They were asked what they are.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.800
None of them gave a thermometer.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:24.400
This film is about the side that is measured, the side that is not, and what would read it.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.000
The Fleet as the Hearth.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.600
The fifteenth of November:

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:36.200
reading the house's air, what the mesh radios can tell, and what to buy.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.400
Begin from far away.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.000
There are four territories.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.800
The Mac keeps the queue, and the record of every machine's readings.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.400
Below it, the machines.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.800
The queue's own buttons give them a step, and their temperatures come back to the record.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.400
Beside them, the house:

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.000
two rooms, a fan between them, and the outside.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Every watt the machines draw goes into it as heat.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.200
And on this Mac's USB, three mesh radios.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.200
From the house to the Mac, nothing comes back yet.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.400
There are four borders.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.800
From the Mac to a machine:

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.000
the queue's own buttons, Pause and then Resume.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.400
From a machine to the Mac:

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.000
one line every five seconds, pulled every five minutes.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.400
From a machine to the house:

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.800
every watt it draws, as heat.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.600
And from the house to the Mac:

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:35.000
nothing.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.400
No thermometer stands in a room.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.800
First, the machines' side, measured that day.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:43.600
Six arrows.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:49.000
The queue's own buttons made the steps.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:55.400
For each machine in turn its CPU lanes were paused, and the job in hand ended.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:59.400
The CPUs sat idle twenty minutes, then took work again.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.800
A card's lane kept working throughout.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.200
On each Linux machine a collector writes one line every five seconds.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.400
It also takes in anything a reader leaves for it in one folder.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.200
So a new probe on a Linux machine needs only a reader.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.800
This Mac and the Mac mini have no collector.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:30.200
Every five minutes the Mac pulls the new lines into its record.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.800
When the report was written, that record was four and a half hours long.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.200
A CPU's tau is a minute.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.200
A disk's is an hour.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.200
Paul said of the disks:

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:45.000
that will be longer, days of data.

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.600
From each step, three numbers.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:50.000
Tau:

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.000
how long the part takes.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.200
Degrees a watt:

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:55.200
how badly it sheds heat.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.400
Joules a degree:

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.800
how much stuff warms with it.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:00.400
Boburner's CPU, twenty-eight seconds.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.400
Saskatchewan's, a hundred and fifteen.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.600
These are one step each, and they move.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:10.800
A figure here is a first reading, good to a factor of two.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:16.200
A disk never gets a clean step.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:23.800
So each part that is not a CPU is fitted to its machine's wall watts, passed through one lag.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:28.600
On bosporus the memory follows the watts by nine to twelve minutes.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.200
Its disks, by thirty-three minutes to over two hours.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.800
Over two hours means the true figure is longer.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.600
All of it is drawn in two places.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.400
One browser page, made at every pull.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.400
And Orrery's Heat, four panels:

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.000
Machines, Parts, Watts, Steps.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:50.400
The panels were checked as pictures.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.000
They were not clicked through in the running program.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.200
Now the three radios.

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:03.000
Three arrows, and no thermometer at the end of any of them.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:12.400
Each radio was opened at a hundred and fifteen thousand two hundred baud, and sent read-only text.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.000
The first T-Echo runs MeshCore as a room server.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.800
Asked for its sensors, it lists one:

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:19.600
its GPS.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:20.400
No thermometer.

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.200
Either the board has no BME280, or this build left it out.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.800
The second T-Echo answers nothing.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:35.800
Not to text, and not to one frame of the companion protocol, sent from memory.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.000
That proves nothing:

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.400
the frame may have been wrong.

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.400
The tool that speaks that protocol is not installed here.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.600
The T-Deck answers every MeshCore command, and help, with:

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:50.400
unknown command.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.800
What it runs was not identified.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:59.200
The sensors repository Paul found is for another board, and names no temperature or humidity chip.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.400
No radio was reflashed, configured or written to.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:06.200
And the house.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:07.000
Seven arrows.

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:12.200
Every one of them is a model, or a thing still to do.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.000
Now the house.

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:20.800
Every watt a computer draws leaves it as heat into the room.

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:26.400
So the fleet's wall watts are the heater's output, and that much is known.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:32.400
What else warms the house, the sun, and what the house loses to the outside:

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.800
none of it is measured yet.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:42.200
There are two rooms, and a fan between them mixing their air.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:45.400
In the model the fan is one number:

00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:49.000
small when it runs, large when it does not.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Each room has its own loss to the outside, and that one number joins them.

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:02.400
A room loses heat by how far it is above the outside.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.600
Sun through the windows is a second heater.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:08.000
Wind and damp change the loss.

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.400
Humidity does not enter the equations.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.800
It matters to comfort, and to the cost of any cooling.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:20.400
Light and wind would come from a weather feed, which nothing here asks today.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.800
How to get a thermometer, soonest first.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.800
Ask saskatchewan's two Geiger counters, which have a temperature command that radbeeper does not send.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.000
Wire a sensor board to the T-Echo here:

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.400
a few dollars and four wires.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:41.400
Use the mesh as a mesh, which was not tried.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:42.600
Or USB probes.

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:46.600
Prices were not checked, except where a source is cited.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.600
Where to put them, for two rooms mixed by a fan.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:58.000
One in each room, at sitting height, away from the machines.

00:07:58.000 --> 00:07:60.000
One outside, in the shade.

00:07:60.000 --> 00:08:02.400
The fleet's intake and its exhaust.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.000
One at the fan.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.600
Six probes cover it.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:14.400
A reader writes each reading into one folder, and the collector, the page and Orrery take it up with no other change.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.800
The fleet is its own step generator.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:25.000
The queue can drop a kilowatt in a minute and put it back.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:35.000
On a still night with the heating off, a Pause of every lane is a step of about one point two kilowatts into the house.

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:39.400
The rooms' fall over the next hours is the house's tau.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:44.600
This is the CPU step test again, with a house for the part.

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.600
It has not been run.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:55.200
With those numbers known, which machines work is a choice of where the heat goes.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.600
The queue already has the dials:

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.400
Pause a machine, set a lane's CPUs.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.200
A controller would hold each room at its mark by choosing them.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:06.800
No controller is built.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.800
None can be, before a thermometer stands in each room.

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:16.200
What was done, and what was not.

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.200
Each CPU's time constant, from a step the queue made.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:23.800
One step each, good to a factor of two.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.400
Each disk's and board's lag behind its machine's watts.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.600
One page and four panels, checked as pictures.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:32.600
No room temperature was read.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:35.800
Every statement about the house is a model.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:40.600
The three radios gave no thermometer, and nothing was written to them.

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:45.000
Two of them were not identified, and no probe was bought.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.800
No controller, dial or throttle is built.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:53.200
The machines are read every five seconds.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:56.400
The house's air is read by nothing today.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.400
What was built is the machines' side of the balance.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:05.600
The house's side is a model, and a list of what to buy.

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:11.600
No controller is built, and none can be before a thermometer stands in each room.
